    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i just had to do a rumenotomy on one of my heifers because of a blockage. can u give me a list of what i should be looking for in terms of recovery and signs of infection over the next week? also what should i feed her once she starts acting normal again?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Post-rumenotomy, monitor your heifer closely for any signs of infection or complications. Key indicators to watch for include:\n\n1. Abnormal swelling, discharge, or redness around the incision site.\n2. Elevated temperature or signs of fever.\n3. Lack of appetite or changes in dry or ruminal bulk.\n\nTo promote recovery and a return to normal feeding, gradually reintroduce feed starting with highly digestible grains like corn silage, oats, or barley. Small, frequent meals are ideal. Ensure her water intake is ample to support healing, and introduce phytase supplements to aid digestion and improve phosphorus availability in her feed. Monitor her weight and continue so they should balanced and not excessively fattened. Always contact your veterinarian if you observe signs of concern.",
      "source": "target",
